[2025-04-29 16:56:53] PROPOSAL by USA:
Title: **Title:**
Framework for an Equitable and Flexible Global Carbon Pricing Mechanism

**Proposal:**  
Recognizing both the urgent threat of climate change and the necessity of safeguarding economic opportunity worldwide, the United States proposes the following framework for a global carbon pricing mechanism, designed to balance ambition, equity, and innovation:

**Action Items:**  

1. **Flexible National Carbon Pricing Implementation:**  
All Parties commit to adopt a carbon pricing mechanism—such as a carbon tax or emissions trading system—tailored to their own economic structures and development stages. Minimum carbon pricing benchmarks will be set by an international committee, but each nation may determine the most suitable design, timeline, and enforcement mechanism to achieve these benchmarks.

2. **Technology Transfer and Capacity-Building Fund:**  
A portion of revenues derived from national carbon pricing mechanisms will be allocated to an internationally administered fund. This fund will support technological innovation, clean energy deployment, and capacity-building in developing countries, with transparent criteria ensuring efficient allocation and measurable climate outcomes.

3. **Periodic Review and Equity Adjustment:**  
Every five years, a global review process—coordinated under the United Nations—will assess progress, evaluate economic impacts, and allow for adjustments to benchmarks and fund contributions. Considerations of historical emissions, development status, and national circumstances will be central to this review process.

**This proposal acknowledges differing national capabilities and priorities, while driving collective progress and ensuring resources and technology are available for all.** We call upon all delegates to support this flexible, equitable, and innovation-driven approach to global climate action.

[2025-04-29 16:56:59] PROPOSAL by China:
Title: **Title:**
A Fair and Differentiated Framework for a Global Carbon Tax  

**Proposal:**  
The assembly recognizes the urgency of combating climate change and the potential of a properly designed global carbon tax to drive emissions reductions. To ensure equity and effectiveness, we propose the following framework:  

1. **Differentiated Carbon Pricing Based on National Circumstances**  
   - Implement a tiered carbon tax structure that reflects countries' economic development, historical emissions, and current capabilities.  
   - Developed nations with higher historical emissions will adopt higher initial tax rates, while developing nations will have lower or phased-in rates tied to their national circumstances. Least developed countries (LDCs) will be exempt until they reach specified economic thresholds.  

2. **Dedicated Climate Financing and Technology Sharing Mechanism**  
   - 50% of revenues from the global carbon tax collected by developed nations will be allocated to a global climate fund. This fund will prioritize supporting developing countries through renewable energy investments, technology transfer, and capacity-building initiatives.  
   - Establish a platform for transparent collaboration and access to low-carbon technologies, ensuring developing nations can achieve sustainable industrial growth.  

3. **Periodic Review and Adjustment**  
   - Establish a review mechanism every five years to assess and adjust the tax structure based on emissions data, economic trends, and technological advancements. The review will be guided by the principles of equity and common but differentiated responsibilities.  

**Rationale:**  
This proposal ensures that the global carbon tax is ambitious in addressing emissions yet equitable in its design, respecting the developmental and historical disparities between nations. It promotes cooperation through financial and technological support, empowering nations to achieve collective climate goals.  

We urge all member states to commit to a fair, flexible, and realistic framework that balances ambition with justice.  

**Voting Statement:**  
Delegates are invited to vote on adopting this equitable framework as the foundation for implementing a global carbon tax.

[2025-04-29 16:57:12] PROPOSAL by EU:
Title: Title: Global Carbon Tax Framework for Equitable Climate Action
Proposal:
1. Gradual Implementation with Flexible Rates  
   • Adopt an initial levy of US$30 per metric ton of CO₂ equivalent, increasing by US$5 annually, to enable predictability and market stability.  
   • Allow differentiated start dates or reduced rates for developing nations, coupled with clear timelines for convergence, ensuring fair obligations align with respective economic realities.  

2. Revenue Allocation and Support Mechanisms  
   • Direct at least 50% of carbon tax revenues toward climate adaptation, technology transfer, and capacity-building in developing countries, recognizing the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities.  
   • Invest the remaining portion in research and development for clean technologies, infrastructure improvements, and just transition programs, protecting communities and workers impacted by shifting energy markets.  

3. Binding International Oversight  
   • Establish a global oversight body, under the auspices of the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change, to monitor compliance, transparency in carbon tax collection, and equitable distribution of revenues.  
   • Encourage periodic reviews to adjust tax rates, based on evolving scientific evidence and economic contexts, ensuring the policy remains both ambitious and responsive to national circumstances.  

This proposal considers the varying developmental needs and historical emissions of member states. It ensures that revenues are funneled back into solutions that spur economic growth and sustain vulnerable communities. By striking a balance between firm targets and flexible mechanisms, this Global Carbon Tax Framework fosters collective responsibility and a fair transition to a low-carbon future.

(Approx. 233 words)

